.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO 3 "2026-07-11" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do \- do an HTTP request and buffer the full response
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_do(chttpcli cli,
                             const chttp_request_t *req,
                             chttpcli_response **resp_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function takes a pool slot, blocking while every slot is in use.
It then runs the HTTP request synchronously, buffers the whole response
body and stores a heap-allocated chttpcli_response in *resp_out.

*resp_out is set to NULL first, before any other work, and it stays
NULL on every return that is not a success. So you can always call
chttpclient_resp_free(resp) after this call, whatever ccol_retval_t it
returns, and you don't have to initialize your own pointer before the
call. After a success the caller owns *resp_out and must free it with
chttpclient_resp_free when done.

The client follows redirects automatically. The redirect codes are
301, 302, 303, 307 and 308, and the client follows up to 50 hops. For
301, 302 and 303 it turns the method into a GET with no body, but
keeps HEAD as HEAD; for 307 and 308 it keeps the original method and
body. The Location header can be an absolute URL, a protocol-relative
reference ("//host/path"), an absolute-path reference ("/foo"), or a
general relative reference such as "foo", "../foo", "./foo" or
"?query", and the client resolves all of them as RFC 3986 describes. A
Location value with a scheme of its own is always absolute, as RFC 3986
SS5.2.2 says, for example "mailto:x@y", "ftp://host/path" or any
scheme other than http, https and http+unix. Such a value resolves to
itself unchanged, and since this client only connects over http, https
and http+unix, the next hop reports ccol_http_invalid_url, the same
code that an unsupported scheme in the original request URL gets. The
client never merges such a reference onto the current origin's path as
if it were relative. A response with a redirect code and no Location
header, or an empty one, is not a redirect, and the client delivers it
as the final response, as the Go net/http client does.

A redirect from https to http is followed by default. That is what
curl does, whose CURLOPT_REDIR_PROTOCOLS permits both schemes, and what
the Go net/http client does.

Set chttp_request_t.prevent_tls_downgrade_on_redirect to refuse such a
redirect instead. The client then reports ccol_http_invalid_url and
opens no plaintext connection. Weigh that against the default: the
peer that sent the Location picks the downgrade, and once a chain
leaves TLS, that hop and every later hop travel in clear text.

A Location that only inherits the scheme, such as "//host/path" or
"/path", resolves back to https and follows whatever that field says,
and so does an upgrade from http to https, which can only add
protection.

The AF_UNIX rule below is not optional, and no field relaxes it.

A hop can name a Unix domain socket ("http+unix://...") only when the
hop that carried the Location was bound to that same socket path.
Every other resolved http+unix:// target is refused with
ccol_http_invalid_url and no connection is tried. The rule holds on
every hop, judged against the hop that the Location came from, so a
chain that leaves a socket cannot come back to it and a chain that
never used a socket cannot reach one, however long the chain is. The
scheme is compared without regard to case, and the socket path is
compared after its percent escapes are decoded, so a different
spelling or a double encoding can't get around the rule. A relative
Location on a Unix hop resolves back onto that hop's socket, and the
client follows it normally. Without this rule any http or https server
could redirect a caller onto any local socket and control the target
of the request as well.

If the client reaches the cap of 50 hops and the last hop's response
is itself a redirect, the client neither follows it nor delivers it,
and returns ccol_http_too_many_redirects instead. The normalization of
the dot segments ".." and "." rewrites only the path; the client never
treats "/", ".." or "." inside a query string as path navigation. When
the request URL carries credentials (see below), the client builds an
Authorization header from them and sends it again on every hop that
stays on the same origin. An origin is a scheme, a host and a port:
hosts are compared without regard to case, schemes and ports exactly.
The client drops that header for good the first time a hop changes
the origin.

The headers that you set with chttp_request_set_header follow a
redirect with two exceptions, which are curl's rules. Every hop is
judged against the ORIGINAL request, never against the hop before it.
A hop whose scheme, host or port differs from the original request
carries none of your Authorization, Cookie, Cookie2 and
WWW-Authenticate headers. A hop whose host differs also carries no
Host header of yours, and the client writes its own Host line for the
new target instead. Hosts and header names are compared without regard
to case. So a hop that comes back to the original origin carries those
headers again, while a second hop on the same foreign origin carries
none of them. Every other header of yours follows every hop.

A 301, 302 or 303 hop turns the method into a GET with no body, and
removes every Content-Length, Content-Type and Expect header that the
caller set, from that hop and from every hop after it. A later 307 or
308 hop can bring a real body back, and the client then sends those
headers again. Those three headers only describe a body, and a value
that described the original request's body is never right on a request
that has no body at all. Every other header of the caller goes out
again on every hop, unchanged.

The request URL accepts http://, https:// and http+unix://, and Unix
domain sockets are supported the same way in all three tiers. The form
with the encoded socket path is "http+unix://%2Fpath%2Fto%2Fsock/path",
the convention of Python's requests-unixsocket. For the two network
forms the client accepts a plain hostname, an IPv4 literal, and an IPv6
literal in brackets ("https://[::1]:8443/path"). A URL can also carry
credentials ("http://user:pass@host/path"), which the client turns into
an "Authorization: Basic ..." header for you, unless the request sets
its own Authorization header. This doesn't apply to http+unix://,
which has no convention for userinfo. The client also strips a
"#fragment" from the end of a URL, because it never sends a fragment
to a server.

An empty port, as in "http://host:/", means the scheme's default port
(RFC 3986 section 3.2.3), as in curl, and port 0 is refused. A fully
qualified hostname with one trailing dot, as in
"https://www.example.com./", is resolved and sent in the Host header
exactly as written, while TLS uses the name without that dot for SNI
and for the certificate check, as curl and Go do. The keep-alive cache
compares hosts without regard to case, but a name with a trailing dot
and the same name without it are two different origins, because the
name without the dot can resolve through a search domain to another
host.

A server can send one response header name on several lines. See
chttpclient_resp_header for the combined value, and
chttpclient_resp_header_at for each line on its own. A redirect with
more than one Location line goes to the first one, as curl and the Go
net/http client do.

The client builds the request-target of the request line from the
path and query of the URL, and from those of every Location it
follows. It percent-encodes as "%XX" every byte that RFC 3986 does not
allow there literally: the space, the control bytes, DEL, every byte
of 0x80 and above, and the characters '"', '<', '>', '\\', '^', '`',
\&'{', '|', '}', '[' and ']'. An existing "%XX" escape is kept as it is
and never encoded twice. A '%' that is not followed by two hex digits
makes the URL invalid, in your URL and in a Location alike, and Go's
net/url rejects the same input. For example, a Location of "/my file"
becomes the request-target "/my%20file".

A host name can resolve to several addresses. The client connects with
Happy Eyeballs (RFC 8305), as Go's default dialer does. It takes the
addresses in the order the resolver returns them, which is the order
of RFC 6724, and alternates the address families, starting with the
family of the first address. The first address is tried at once, and
the next one when the attempts in flight have given no result for
250 ms, or at once when an attempt fails. The first attempt that
connects wins and every other one is closed; ccol_http_connection_failed
is reported only after every address has failed. The connect timeout
is one budget for the whole race, and when it runs out the connect
ends with ccol_timed_out. So an address that drops its packets costs
about 250 ms instead of the whole connect timeout, and the name
"localhost", which a standard /etc/hosts lists as ::1 before
127.0.0.1, reaches a server that listens on 127.0.0.1 only.
Every tier starts the next address 250 ms after the one before it.

A server can answer before it has read the full request, for example
with a 401 from a credentials check or a 413 from a size limit, and
then close the connection or stop reading. Every tier watches the
connection for such an answer while it sends the request, and returns
that answer, as curl and Go's net/http client do. The rules are:
  - An interim (1xx) response is dropped and the send goes on.
  - A final response of 300 or above stops the send once its header
    block has arrived, and the rest of the request is not sent.
  - A final response of 200 to 299 lets the send go on while the
    response arrives, because a server can stream its answer while it
    reads the request. A complete response stops the send.
  - A write that fails because the peer went away stops the send, and
    the client then returns the answer that the peer sent before it
    went, if there is one.
The client never reuses a connection whose response started before it
had sent the full request.

On Linux a reset throws away data that the client has not read yet, so
the client can return an answer only if it reads it before the reset
arrives. chttpclient_do waits for readability during every wait of the
send. chttpclient_do_async and chttpclient_do_pooled look for an answer
at these points:
  - When a write would block.
  - After a write, once 32 KiB or more went out since the last look.
A response that arrives while a write of chttpclient_do_async or
chttpclient_do_pooled waits for room in the socket is seen within
100 ms.

Every socket of the client is close-on-exec, and so is every file that
the client's TLS layer reads, the system CA bundle included, so a
process that the application starts inherits no connection of the
client. The one exception is a system with no default CA file, where
OpenSSL itself opens the files of its hashed certificate directory
during a handshake; see chttpclient_set_tls(3).

A server can send interim informational responses with a 1xx status.
The client drops each of them and keeps reading on the same connection
for the real response, so a caller never gets a 1xx status as the
final answer. RFC 8297's "103 Early Hints" is the most frequent
example, and it can come before an ordinary response whatever the
value of req->expect_continue.

The client does not accept an endless sequence of interim responses.
Once it has dropped 64 interim responses in a row on one hop,
chttpclient_do stops and returns ccol_http_transfer_aborted. The limit
matters because the request timeout is 0 by default, and 0 means no
timeout.

The client does not drop "101 Switching Protocols". A 101 answers an
Upgrade request, which this client never sends, and the bytes after a
101 belong to another protocol, so a 101 fails the request with
ccol_http_transfer_aborted in every tier, as in curl.

One header line of a response can be up to 64 KiB long and the whole
header block up to 64 KiB and 100 lines, while the status line must
fit in 8 KiB. A response past any of those limits gives
ccol_http_transfer_aborted, and the same limits apply in every tier.

A response with no Content-Length and no chunked framing has a body
that ends when the server closes the connection. Over https:// the
body also ends there when the server closes without a TLS close_notify
alert, which curl and the Go net/http client accept too, because many
servers close that way; a close in the middle of a TLS record is
ccol_http_transfer_aborted. Such a body can't be told apart from one
that a failure cut off at a record boundary, so a server whose
responses must arrive whole should send a Content-Length or chunked
framing.

If req->expect_continue is true and the request has a body, the client
first sends only the request headers, then waits up to one second for
the first byte of a response. That second bounds only this wait: a
response that starts inside it is read to the end, bounded only by the
request timeout, however long the rest of it takes. The wait ends in
one of these ways:

  - The server sends a "100 Continue" interim response. The client
    then sends the body and reads the final response normally. A
    response that arrives together with the "100 Continue" is read
    before the body goes out, under the rules for an answer that
    arrives while the request is being sent (see above): a final
    response of 300 or above, or a complete one, ends the request
    without a byte of the body sent.
  - The server sends some OTHER interim 1xx status during the wait,
    such as "103 Early Hints" before "100 Continue". The client drops
    it and keeps waiting for the first byte of the next response,
    within the same one-second budget.
  - The server answers with a final status instead, for example to
    reject the request and ask for no body. The client hands that
    response to the caller as it is and never sends the body, and it
    closes the connection afterwards and never reuses it. This also
    holds for a final response that is still arriving when the second
    runs out.
  - The second runs out before any byte of a response. The client then
    assumes that the server doesn't support this mechanism, sends the
    body and reads the final response normally.

The cap of 64 dropped interim responses in a row counts separately in
two phases, the wait for "100 Continue" and the read of the final
response after it. A "100 Continue" is a real outcome and so is the
end of the wait without a response; neither counts as a dropped
response, and each one resets the count to zero. So one hop can drop
up to 64 interim responses while it waits for "100 Continue" and up to
64 more while it reads the final response, and the total is not 64.

expect_continue has no effect on a request without a body. Every tier
honors it the same way: chttpclient_do_async and everything built on it
use the same three-outcome protocol described above, but as part of
their own non-blocking dispatch instead of a blocking wait.

A connection from the keep-alive idle pool can turn out to be dead only
after the client has written the request on it. When nothing came back,
the client sends the request once more on a new connection, but only
when the connection did not take the whole request, or when the
request is idempotent: a GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, PUT or DELETE, or any
request with an Idempotency-Key or X-Idempotency-Key header. A POST or
PATCH without either header that went out in full may have run on the
server already, so the call fails with ccol_http_transfer_aborted
instead and the server receives the request once; the application
decides whether to send it again. "Connection reuse" in chttpclient(7)
has the full rule.

Once the client has received a real "100 Continue" interim response
and written the body on that connection, it reports any later failure
on that connection directly and never tries the request again on
another connection, whatever the method. The server showed that it was
alive and accepted the body, so a retry would send the body twice.

A request whose Connection header lists the "close" option is the last
one on its connection. The client closes the connection after the
response, whatever the response says (RFC 9112 section 9.6), and the
next request to that origin opens a new one.

The buffered response body has no size limit by default. Call
chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size to cap it, and the client then
returns ccol_msg_too_large for a response over that cap instead of
buffering the whole body.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli       The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
req       The request to run. It must not be NULL.
resp_out  The output parameter. It must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success
       The request finished, and *resp_out is valid.

ccol_invalid_args
       An argument is NULL; or cli is CHTTPCLI_INVALID, or a stale
       handle of a client that has been destroyed; or req->method
       names no HTTP method (CHTTP_ANY, or a value outside the enum,
       set after construction); or a request header value holds a
       control byte other than a horizontal tab (any byte below 0x20,
       CR and LF among them, or 0x7F), and the same for
       body.content_type on a request with a body; or a request
       header name is not a token; or a request header has the name
       "Transfer-Encoding"; or, on a request that carries a body
       (POST, PUT or PATCH), a request header named "Content-Length"
       holds a bad value. A valid value is a plain unsigned string of
       decimal digits, with no leading '+' or '-' and no whitespace in
       it, and it must also match the real length of the body that
       the client sends. See chttp_request_set_header(3).

ccol_not_enough_memory
       A memory allocation failed. When that happens while the client
       checks a redirect target against the rules in DESCRIPTION, the
       client follows no further hop and reports this code, so a
       check that could not finish never lets a hop through.

ccol_timed_out
       The request timeout or the connect timeout fired.

ccol_not_permitted
       The client is being destroyed and accepts no new request.

ccol_http_invalid_url
       The URL is malformed; or it uses an unsupported scheme (the
       client supports only http://, https:// and http+unix://); or
       its host, port or userinfo part is missing or not valid.

       The host must match the uri-host rule of RFC 3986 section
       3.2.2, which means it must be one of these:
         - A name of letters, digits, '-', '.', '_', '~', the
           sub-delims !$&'()*+,;= and %XX escapes.
         - A bracketed IPv6 address or IPvFuture literal.
       A space, a tab, a control byte, a byte of 0x80 or above, a
       backslash, and a bracket that the authority does not close all
       make the URL invalid. Write a host name outside ASCII in its
       punycode (IDNA) form. IPv6 zone identifiers are not
       supported.

       Credentials in the URL can hold only the characters of the
       userinfo rule, %XX escapes and an unescaped '@' in the
       password. A user name that holds a ':' once its escapes are
       decoded, as in "http://a%3Ab:pw@host/", has no valid Basic
       credential (see chttp_basic_auth(3)), so such a URL is invalid
       as well.

       A CR or LF byte in the path or the query, and a '%' in the path
       or the query that is not followed by two hex digits, also make
       the URL invalid. Without these checks the client would copy
       such a byte into the Host header or the request line, and the
       URL could then inject extra header lines or smuggle a whole
       second request. Every Location that the client follows goes
       through the same rules.

       Or, for http+unix://, the URL gives a socket path that is too
       long for sockaddr_un.sun_path. The client also returns this
       code when a redirect resolves to an http+unix:// target and the
       hop that carried the Location was not bound to it (see
       DESCRIPTION).

ccol_http_host_resolution_failed
       The DNS resolution of the target host failed. The client never
       returns this code for http+unix://, which has no DNS step.

ccol_http_connection_failed
       The client could not connect: every address of the host
       refused or failed, or an http+unix:// socket path does not
       exist. A socket error on a connection that is already
       established is ccol_http_transfer_aborted instead.

ccol_http_too_many_redirects
       The redirect chain went past 50 hops.

ccol_http_tls_handshake_failed
       The TLS handshake failed for a reason other than certificate
       verification. A peer that resets or closes the connection
       after the TCP connect and before the handshake finishes gives
       this code, in every tier.

ccol_http_tls_cert_verification_failed
       The client could not verify the peer's certificate or its
       hostname.

ccol_http_tls_cert_load_failed
       The client could not read the client certificate, the key or
       the CA bundle path of the configuration, or the TLS layer could
       not load or parse one of them.

ccol_http_transfer_aborted
       The connection failed during the transfer, or the server sent
       a malformed HTTP/1.1 response. A reused connection that took
       the whole of a non-idempotent request and then died without a
       byte of a response also gives this code (see DESCRIPTION). A
       response with ambiguous framing is malformed, and these
       responses have ambiguous framing:
         - A response with both a Content-Length and a
           Transfer-Encoding (RFC 7230 section 3.3.3).
         - A response that applies "chunked" more than once
           (section 3.3.1). The client reads the codings of all
           Transfer-Encoding lines as one list.
       The client decodes the "chunked" transfer coding and no other,
       and never asks for another one, so a final response whose
       Transfer-Encoding names any other coding (gzip, deflate,
       identity, ...) fails with this code instead of returning a
       body that is still encoded, as the Go net/http client does. A
       chunk-size line is also malformed when its chunk extension
       does not follow the grammar of RFC 9112 section 7.1.1, for
       example when it holds a control byte.

       Or a streaming chttpcli_write_fn returned a value other than
       the number of bytes it was given.

       Over https://, this code also covers a connection that closes
       in the middle of a TLS record.

       A connection that fails while the client sends the request
       gives this code only when the client found no answer from the
       server; see DESCRIPTION. A "101 Switching Protocols" response
       also gives this code.

ccol_msg_too_large
       The response body went past the cap set by
       chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size. The client returns
       this code only once you have set that cap.

ccol_unexpected_failure
       Any other internal failure that the codes above do not cover.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library leaves the SIGPIPE disposition to the application and
never changes it, in any tier. No write of the client raises that
signal, because every socket write, TLS records and the close_notify of
a TLS teardown included, is a send(2) with MSG_NOSIGNAL. A server that
closes or resets the connection while the client writes a request makes
that request fail and never ends the process.
.fi
